# Lesson 4, new exercise: QJT95 opposite A432 at no-trump. Lead the queen from dummy; giving up a diamond to East would let West run the spades.
title: Lead the queen through East
id: L04-12
type: exercise
lesson: 4
concepts: 4.2, 3.5
student: S
board: 78
dealer: E
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  942    Q7     QJT95  A63
  E  T5     J653   K87    KJ42
  S  A73    AK4    A432   Q87
  W  KQJ86  T982   6      T95

auction:
  E Pass
  S 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
     not *: 17 points and a balanced hand: what does the lesson open?
  W Pass
  N 2NT    = 8-9 points, balanced: inviting game
  E Pass
  S 3NT    = A maximum: accept
     not Pass: You have 17, the top of your range. Accept the invitation.
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  You opened 1NT, partner invited with 2NT, and with 17 you accepted. You are
  declarer, because you bid no-trump first, and so West will lead to the
  first trick.

planning:
  winners: 9-10
  spades: 1 = The ace.
  hearts: 3 = Your ace and king, and dummy's queen. Play the queen early, the high card from the short side.
  diamonds: 4-5 = Four once the king is gone. All five if East has the king and you lead from dummy.
  clubs: 1 = Dummy's ace.
  remind: You need 9 tricks. Count the obvious winners in each suit: spades, hearts, diamonds, clubs.
  remind: Your ♠A is your only spade stopper. What happens if you lose the lead?
play:
  1   W SK  N S2  E S5  S SA
      note: Nine winners if you can set up the diamonds, but once your ♠A is gone, West's spades are all winners. You can't afford to lose a diamond trick to the king. Win the ♠A.
  2   S H4  W H2  N HQ  E H3
      note: Over to dummy with the ♥Q, to lead diamonds from there.
  3   N DQ  E D7  S D2*  W D6
      note: Lead the ♦Q from dummy.
      D2 key: Let the queen run. If East has the king, the queen wins and you are still in dummy.
      D2 not DA: Did you think about letting the queen run? With the ace, you'll have to lose a diamond to the king, and then West takes four spades. If East has the king, the queen wins and you never lose the lead.
  4   N DJ  E D8  S D3*  W C5
      note: The queen won, and West shows out. East still has the king. Lead the jack.
      D3 key: Let it run again. East's king is trapped.
      D3 not DA: Did you notice East still has the king? Let the jack run.
  5   N DT  E DK  S DA  W C9
      note: Lead the ten. East covers with the king, and your ace takes it.
  6   S D4  W H8  N D9  E C2
      note: Keep a low diamond in your hand to reach dummy's last two.
  7   N D5  E C4  S S3  W S6
      note: Dummy's last diamond.
  8   N H7  E H5  S HA  W H9
      note: Back to your hand for the hearts.
  9   S HK  W HT  N S4  E H6
      note: That's nine tricks. Cash the ♣A too.
  10  S C7  W CT  N CA  E CJ
      note: Ten.
  11  N C6  E CK  S C8  W S8
      note: Nothing left to win. Give up the rest.
  12  E HJ  S CQ  W SJ  N S9
  13  E ST  S S7  W SQ  N C3
      note: 10 tricks: 3NT made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: you had nine tricks once the diamonds were set up, but you could
  not afford to let East in with the ♦K, because East would lead a spade and
  West would cash four.

  Leading the queen, then the jack, kept you in dummy whenever they won, and
  you could lead honors because you held all the cards in between. With the
  king in East, you never lost a diamond. If West had held the king, you
  would have lost it to West, and West would have cashed the spades.
